### 7.4 Studies on the systematic uncertainty

The error bars shown in the previous plots are statistical only. Additional errors need to be considered regarding the systematics of our analysis. In this section I will give a brief discussion of the systematics that will affect our measurements.

#### Uncorrelated background

The main source of the systematic uncertainty is due to the uncertainty of the magnitude of the uncorrelated background. Since I am using the calculated uncorrelated background from the convolution of the single particle distributions, the uncertainty of our calculation relies on the accuracy of our measurement of the single particle distributions, in particular the fluctuation in the multiplicity for different centrality bins can cause a trigger bias. It has been found that high \(p_{T}\) trigger particles are more likely to be found in central events with high multiplicity. However the fluctuations in central collisions are not significant as is shown in Table 7.1. In my analysis, I am using the difference of the fitted background and the calculation as the systematic error, which should be considered the upper limit of systematic uncertainty. For the most central collisions such uncertainty are less than.5%. However because the magnitude of the background is much larger than jet-like correlations, the effect on the same side yield is about 11% as shown in Table7.1.

#### Leading particle bias

Another effect in our measurements is the leading particle bias. An event could contain a second high particle which exceed the transverse momentum of the trigger particle. However such an effect is usually negligible due to its low probability. Figure 30 show the comparison of the correlations before and after the correction for trigger. The estimated systematic error is less than 1.4%.

