## Appendix B Momentum conservation in azimuthal correlations

Generally, for a system of \(N\) uncorrelated particles with momenta \(p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{N}\), with their components in the transverse direction as \(p_{T1},p_{T2},\ldots,p_{TN}\), from conservation of momentum we have \(\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{i}=Const.\). Since we are only interested in azimuthal correlation which relate to the momentum conservation in the transverse plane, the condition is described as following,

\[\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{T}^{N}=0.\] (B.0.1)

For any sub group of \(k\) particles, the probability distribution of the momentum \(p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{k}\) can be expressed in terms of product of the single particle distributions.

\[f_{c}(p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{k})\equiv\frac{\left(\prod_{i=1}^{k}\not{\!f}(p_{i })\int\delta^{2}(\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{T}^{N})\prod_{j=k+1}^{N}\left(\not{\!f}(p_{j} )d^{3}p_{j}\right)\right.}{\int\delta^{2}(\sum_{i=1}^{N}p_{T}^{N})\prod_{n=1}^{ N}\left(\not{\!f}(p_{n})d^{3}p_{n}\right)}\] (B.0.2)

Where the \(f(p)\) denotes the normalized single particle momentum distribution. In order to calculate \(f_{c}\) we use the following approximation. Let \(P_{T}=\sum_{i=1}^{M}p_{Ti}\). When \(M\) is large, according to the central limit theorem we would expect a Gaussian

